---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
---
<Base
  title="NVL72 vs HGX vs DGX"
  description="Scale-up against scale-out, what actually differs between a rack-scale system and an 8-GPU server, and a roofline that explains why bandwidth beats FLOPS here."
  kicker="Chapter 09"
  heading="NVL72 vs HGX vs DGX"
  slug="/comparison"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      The differences that matter are not on the spec sheet's first page. Per-GPU NVLink bandwidth
      is the same in both designs. What changed is how many GPUs that bandwidth reaches, what sits
      between the GPU and its host, and what you are actually buying.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-differs">What actually differs</h2>

    <div class="spec-table bleed">
      <table>
        <caption>Rack-scale versus server-scale</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">&nbsp;</th>
            <th scope="col" style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">GB200 NVL72</th>
            <th scope="col" style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">HGX / DGX 8-GPU node</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">GPUs in one NVLink domain</th><td>72</td><td>8</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">NVLink bandwidth per GPU</th><td>1.8 TB/s</td><td>1.8 TB/s</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Host CPU</th><td>Grace, Arm Neoverse V2</td><td>x86</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">CPU ↔ GPU link</th><td>NVLink-C2C, 900 GB/s, coherent</td><td>PCIe</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Pooled HBM in the domain</th><td>~13.5 TB</td><td>~1.5 TB</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Cooling</th><td>Liquid, mandatory</td><td>Air or liquid</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Power delivery</th><td>Rack busbar, shared</td><td>Per-server PSUs</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Unit of purchase</th><td>Rack, or Superchip</td><td>Server</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Unit of integration</th><td>The rack</td><td>The baseboard</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Read the first two rows together and the design becomes clear. Per-GPU NVLink bandwidth did
        not change. What changed is that the same bandwidth now reaches 72 peers instead of 8, and
        the pooled memory those peers can address grew by the same factor to
        <S id="hbm-total" />. NVL72 is not a faster interconnect; it is the same interconnect
        stretched over nine times as many endpoints.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other structural change is on the host side. Replacing x86-over-PCIe with
        Grace-over-<T id="nvlink-c2c">NVLink-C2C</T> turns host memory from a staging area into a
        genuine second tier of the same coherent address space — which is why the rack's
        <S id="mem-total" /> of unified memory is quoted as a single figure rather than as HBM plus
        something else.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"You can rent one GPU from it."</strong> Not in the way you rent an A100. These are
        sold and deployed at Superchip or rack granularity, and the value proposition is the domain
        rather than the device. A single Blackwell GPU detached from the fabric is a fine GPU and a
        poor reason to have built any of this.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="roofline">Why bandwidth, not FLOPS</h2>

    <p>
      The <T id="roofline">roofline</T> model says a kernel's achievable throughput is capped by
      either arithmetic or by data movement, whichever binds first, and that which one binds
      depends on a single property of the kernel: how many FLOPs it performs per byte it moves.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Roofline"
    hint="Drag the intensity · hover a workload, click to keep it"
    caption="Log–log. The sloped section is bandwidth-bound, the flat section compute-bound, and the corner is the arithmetic intensity a kernel needs before FLOPS is the constraint. Ceiling is dense FP4 per GPU; switching the bandwidth to NVLink or InfiniBand shows the same argument one tier out, where the operand is a message rather than a cache line."
  >
    <div data-roof>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 340" role="img"
        aria-label="Roofline plot of attainable throughput against arithmetic intensity, with markers for decode, attention prefill and large GEMM workloads.">
        <g data-plot></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Arithmetic intensity</dt><dd data-out="ai">2 FLOP/byte</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Attainable</dt><dd data-out="att">16 TFLOPS</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Share of peak</dt><dd data-out="pct">0.2%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Bound by</dt><dd data-out="bound">memory</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Workload</dt><dd data-out="mark">—</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p data-out="note" style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--ink-2)"></p>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="ai">Arithmetic intensity<output data-out="ai-lbl">2 FLOP/byte</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="ai" data-ai min="0" max="100" value="18" />
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Which bandwidth binds">
        <button type="button" data-bw="hbm" aria-pressed="true">HBM3e · 8 TB/s</button>
        <button type="button" data-bw="nvlink" aria-pressed="false">NVLink · 900 GB/s</button>
        <button type="button" data-bw="ib" aria-pressed="false">InfiniBand · 100 GB/s</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Put Blackwell's numbers in: about <S id="gpu-fp4" /> of sparse FP4, so roughly 10 PFLOPS
        dense, against <S id="hbm-bw" /> of HBM. The ridge point lands north of a thousand FLOPs
        per byte. Dense GEMM with large tiles clears it. <T id="decode">Decode</T>, at an
        arithmetic intensity near one, is three orders of magnitude below it and will never touch
        the flat part of the roof no matter how many FLOPS the chip has.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the whole justification for the rack. Buying more FLOPS does nothing for a
        bandwidth-bound phase. Buying more bandwidth — and, crucially, more <em>places to spread
        the working set</em> so that each GPU reads less — does. A 72-GPU coherent domain is a
        bandwidth purchase disguised as a compute purchase.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        The version of this argument that matters at rack scale replaces HBM bandwidth with fabric
        bandwidth and cache lines with messages. Switch the widget to NVLink and the ridge point
        drops by roughly an order of magnitude; switch it to InfiniBand and it drops again. Any
        model dimension whose per-byte arithmetic falls below the ridge for a given tier is a
        dimension that tier cannot afford to carry.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run that test on an MoE layer's expert all-to-all — a pure data movement with essentially
        no arithmetic attached — and it fails at every tier. It only becomes affordable when the
        bandwidth in the denominator is 900 GB/s per GPU rather than 50 or 100, which is the
        argument <a href="/inference">the inference chapter</a> makes in throughput terms.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Deeper title="Scale-up and scale-out, stated once">
      <p>
        <T id="scale-up">Scale-up</T> makes one machine larger: NVLink, coherent memory, load/store
        semantics, and a hard limit set by physics and power. <em>Scale-out</em> adds more machines:
        InfiniBand or Ethernet, message passing, and a limit set by money and floor space.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every generation of this hardware is an argument about where the boundary between them
        should sit. NVL72's answer is "at the rack". <a href="/roadmap">The roadmap</a> is a series
        of proposals to move it further out — and a matching series of power figures explaining
        what that costs.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    const roof = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-roof]');
    if (roof) {
      const fig = roof.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const plot = roof.querySelector('[data-plot]')!;
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const slider = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-ai]')!;
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };

      const PEAK = 10e15;                 // dense FP4 per GPU, FLOPS
      const BW: Record<string, { label: string; bw: number; note: string }> = {
        hbm: { label: 'HBM3e', bw: 8e12,
          note: 'The classic single-GPU roofline. The ridge sits above 1,000 FLOP/byte, so only large dense GEMMs reach the flat roof.' },
        nvlink: { label: 'NVLink 5 egress', bw: 900e9,
          note: 'The same argument one tier out: the operand is now a message crossing the fabric, not a cache line. The ridge drops by roughly an order of magnitude, which is why a coherent 72-GPU domain can carry traffic a network cannot.' },
        ib: { label: 'InfiniBand', bw: 100e9,
          note: 'Across racks. The ridge drops again, and any exchange with little arithmetic attached — an expert all-to-all, a KV cache transfer — becomes something you schedule around rather than something you simply do.' },
      };
      let mode = 'hbm';

      const X0 = 56, X1 = 700, Y0 = 24, Y1 = 280;
      const AI_MIN = 0.1, AI_MAX = 1e5;
      const F_MIN = 1e11, F_MAX = 2e16;
      const lx = (ai: number) => X0 + (Math.log10(ai / AI_MIN) / Math.log10(AI_MAX / AI_MIN)) * (X1 - X0);
      const ly = (f: number) => Y1 - (Math.log10(f / F_MIN) / Math.log10(F_MAX / F_MIN)) * (Y1 - Y0);
      const aiFromSlider = () => AI_MIN * Math.pow(AI_MAX / AI_MIN, Number(slider.value) / 100);
      const fmtF = (f: number) => f >= 1e15 ? (f / 1e15).toFixed(1) + ' PFLOPS' : f >= 1e12 ? (f / 1e12).toFixed(1) + ' TFLOPS' : (f / 1e9).toFixed(0) + ' GFLOPS';
      const fmtAI = (a: number) => a >= 100 ? Math.round(a).toLocaleString() : a.toFixed(1);

      const MARKS = [
        { ai: 1.2, label: 'decode', note: 'One token at a time, re-reading every weight. Memory-bound at any batch size that fits — more FLOPS buys nothing here.' },
        { ai: 60, label: 'attention prefill', note: 'The whole prompt at once. Better, but still short of the ridge on HBM: attention re-reads the KV cache faster than it accumulates arithmetic.' },
        { ai: 2500, label: 'large GEMM', note: 'Big dense tiles. The one shape that clears the ridge and actually reaches the flat roof this hardware is sold on.' },
      ];
      const MUTED = 0.1;

      // Hover previews a workload, a click keeps it, and touching the slider
      // by hand releases it — the same contract as the other figures.
      let pinnedMark: number | null = null;
      let hoverMark: number | null = null;
      /** The intensity the reader last chose with the slider themselves. */
      let manualValue = slider.value;

      const sliderFor = (ai: number) =>
        String(Math.round(100 * Math.log10(ai / AI_MIN) / Math.log10(AI_MAX / AI_MIN)));

      const render = () => {
        const bw = BW[mode].bw;
        const ridge = PEAK / bw;
        const active = hoverMark ?? pinnedMark;
        // Read the exact marker intensity rather than round-tripping it through
        // the slider's integer steps, which would land the cursor next to the
        // workload it is meant to be sitting on.
        const ai = active !== null ? MARKS[active].ai : aiFromSlider();
        const att = Math.min(PEAK, bw * ai);

        plot.textContent = '';
        // grid + axes
        for (const d of [0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1e3, 1e4, 1e5]) {
          plot.appendChild(el('line', { x1: lx(d), y1: Y0, x2: lx(d), y2: Y1, class: 'grid-line' }));
          const t = el('text', { x: lx(d), y: Y1 + 16, 'text-anchor': 'middle' });
          t.textContent = d >= 1000 ? d / 1000 + 'k' : String(d);
          plot.appendChild(t);
        }
        for (const f of [1e12, 1e13, 1e14, 1e15, 1e16]) {
          plot.appendChild(el('line', { x1: X0, y1: ly(f), x2: X1, y2: ly(f), class: 'grid-line' }));
          const t = el('text', { x: X0 - 8, y: ly(f) + 4, 'text-anchor': 'end' });
          t.textContent = fmtF(f).replace(/\.0/, '');
          plot.appendChild(t);
        }
        plot.appendChild(el('line', { x1: X0, y1: Y0, x2: X0, y2: Y1, class: 'axis' }));
        plot.appendChild(el('line', { x1: X0, y1: Y1, x2: X1, y2: Y1, class: 'axis' }));
        const xl = el('text', { x: (X0 + X1) / 2, y: Y1 + 40, 'text-anchor': 'middle' });
        xl.textContent = 'arithmetic intensity — FLOP per byte';
        plot.appendChild(xl);

        // roof
        plot.appendChild(el('path', {
          d: `M ${lx(AI_MIN)} ${ly(Math.max(F_MIN, bw * AI_MIN))} L ${lx(ridge)} ${ly(PEAK)} L ${lx(AI_MAX)} ${ly(PEAK)}`,
          fill: 'none', stroke: 'var(--copper)', 'stroke-width': 2.5,
        }));
        plot.appendChild(el('line', { x1: lx(ridge), y1: ly(PEAK), x2: lx(ridge), y2: Y1,
          stroke: 'var(--copper)', 'stroke-width': 1, 'stroke-dasharray': '3 4', opacity: 0.6 }));
        const rl = el('text', { x: lx(ridge), y: Y0 - 6, 'text-anchor': 'middle', fill: 'var(--copper)' });
        rl.textContent = `ridge ${fmtAI(ridge)}`;
        plot.appendChild(rl);

        // workload markers
        MARKS.forEach((m, i) => {
          const y = ly(Math.min(PEAK, bw * m.ai));
          const on = active === null || active === i;
          const g = el('g', { 'data-mark': i, tabindex: 0, role: 'button',
            opacity: on ? 1 : MUTED, 'aria-label': `${m.label}, ${fmtAI(m.ai)} FLOP per byte` }) as SVGGElement;
          g.style.cursor = 'pointer';
          g.appendChild(el('circle', { cx: lx(m.ai), cy: y, r: active === i ? 5 : 3.5,
            fill: active === i ? 'var(--gpu)' : 'var(--ink-3)' }));
          const t = el('text', { x: lx(m.ai), y: y - 11, 'text-anchor': 'middle',
            fill: active === i ? 'var(--gpu)' : 'var(--ink-3)' });
          t.textContent = m.label + (i === pinnedMark ? ' ·' : '');
          g.appendChild(t);
          // A generous invisible hit area — the dot alone is a hard target.
          g.appendChild(el('rect', { x: lx(m.ai) - 34, y: y - 22, width: 68, height: 30,
            fill: 'transparent' }));
          plot.appendChild(g);
        });

        // cursor
        plot.appendChild(el('circle', { cx: lx(ai), cy: ly(att), r: 6, fill: 'var(--gpu)' }));
        plot.appendChild(el('line', { x1: lx(ai), y1: ly(att), x2: lx(ai), y2: Y1,
          stroke: 'var(--gpu)', 'stroke-width': 1, 'stroke-dasharray': '2 3' }));

        const bound = ai < ridge ? 'memory' : 'compute';
        out('ai').textContent = fmtAI(ai) + ' FLOP/byte';
        out('ai-lbl').textContent = fmtAI(ai) + ' FLOP/byte';
        out('att').textContent = fmtF(att);
        out('pct').textContent = (att / PEAK * 100).toFixed(att / PEAK < 0.01 ? 2 : 1) + '%';
        out('bound').textContent = bound;
        out('mark').textContent = active === null
          ? '—'
          : MARKS[active].label + (active === pinnedMark ? ' · pinned' : '');
        out('note').textContent = active === null ? BW[mode].note : MARKS[active].note;

        // Wired after each redraw, since the markers are rebuilt every time.
        plot.querySelectorAll<SVGGElement>('[data-mark]').forEach((g) => {
          const i = Number(g.dataset.mark);
          const preview = () => { hoverMark = i; slider.value = sliderFor(MARKS[i].ai); render(); };
          const toggle = () => {
            pinnedMark = pinnedMark === i ? null : i;
            hoverMark = null;
            slider.value = pinnedMark === null ? manualValue : sliderFor(MARKS[i].ai);
            render();
          };
          g.addEventListener('mouseenter', preview);
          g.addEventListener('focus', preview);
          g.addEventListener('click', toggle);
          g.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
            if (e.key !== 'Enter' && e.key !== ' ') return;
            e.preventDefault();
            toggle();
          });
        });
      };

      slider.addEventListener('input', () => {
        // Moving the slider by hand is a deliberate override: drop the pin.
        pinnedMark = null;
        hoverMark = null;
        manualValue = slider.value;
        render();
      });
      plot.closest('svg')?.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
        if (hoverMark === null) return;
        hoverMark = null;
        slider.value = pinnedMark === null ? manualValue : sliderFor(MARKS[pinnedMark].ai);
        render();
      });
      fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-bw]').forEach((b) =>
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          mode = b.dataset.bw!;
          fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-bw]').forEach((o) =>
            o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
          render();
        }));
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
